#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "4bd17973-6b8a-51d6-b4c2-fb941cc1f24d" :question/math-academy-id "e-537" :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n$y' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3$,\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2)$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy = 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 3 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy = 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 6 = 18 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 18 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 9 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 6 + 9 = 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175729 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "344b786f-0c6c-5ea4-9493-c787df2543ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7701" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\,? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute\n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= xy^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\\n&= 40 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 40 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = 20\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "29a57695-57ff-566d-8231-bab1bc99a240" :question/math-academy-id "e-9354" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3)$. The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3$, and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{3} - y^{2} = 0^{3} - 1^{2} =-1 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx =-1 \\cdot 1 =-1 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + (-1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{3} - y^{2} = 1^{3} - 0^{2} = 1 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 1 \\cdot 1 = 1 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 0 + 1 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{3} - y^{2} = 2^{3} - 1^{2} = 7 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 7 \\cdot 1 = 7 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 7 = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(3)\\approx 8\n$$"}]}
